{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795713548,"nonce":"cCOmc0WLZREwkHLYXLouZOWFORs=","title":"Two test_core_device tests fail on main: domain data stays numpy, the tests expect CPU-pinned JAX arrays","message":"Two tests in `tests/test_core_device.py` fail on main, and the cause is a deliberate change in `core.prepare_domain_data` that the tests were never updated for.\n\n**What goes wrong.**\n- `TestDomainDataCPUPinning::test_context_arrays_are_jax_arrays_not_numpy` fails on CPU and on GPU: `domain_data.context['__time__'] is ndarray, expected jax.Array`. The test requires every non-dict, non-string context value to be a `jax.Array` (\"plain numpy would break JAX tracer indexing inside jit'd functions\").\n- `TestGPUPlacement::test_offload_data_full_dataset_stays_on_host` fails on GPU (it is skipped without one): `np.asarray was never called with a jax.Array during offload_data solve`. The test spies on `np.asarray` and expects the offload path to convert CPU-pinned JAX arrays; the context arrays are already numpy, so the spy sees no jax.Array.\n\n**Reproduce.**\n\n    JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu pytest \"tests/test_core_device.py::TestDomainDataCPUPinning::test_context_arrays_are_jax_arrays_not_numpy\"\n    JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu pytest \"tests/test_core_device.py::TestGPUPlacement::test_offload_data_full_dataset_stays_on_host\"\n\nBoth fail on main (CI run: CPU and GPU for the first, GPU for the second). Run in isolation, so not an ordering effect.\n\n**Cause (read from the code and the history, confirmed by the failing assertion).** `git log -S\"a host array is already on the host\"` points to the commit \"Enclosure radiation as an equation, pseudo-transient Newton, region unknowns and fem.solve returning a Solution (#378)\". In `prepare_domain_data` the line `arr = jax.device_put(arr, _cpu_device())` became `if not isinstance(arr, np.ndarray): arr = jax.device_put(...)`, with the comment that pinning a host array would copy it. Every numpy context entry (`__time__` here, and the other sampled coordinates) therefore stays `np.ndarray`. The two tests encode the older contract (everything is a CPU-pinned JAX array).\n\n**Expected.** One contract, stated and tested. Either (a) numpy context is the new contract: update both tests, and check that no jit'd code indexes a numpy context entry with a tracer (that is the failure the old test guarded against; I did not check it); or (b) restore the pinning, accepting the copy. Passing code and tests that disagree about this is the bug.\n\n**Where.** `jno/core.py`: `core.prepare_domain_data` (the `device_put` guard) and the offload branch of `core.solve` (`host_context = {k: ... np.asarray(v)}`); `tests/test_core_device.py` (both tests).\n\n**Workaround.** None needed for users; the suite is red.\n\n**Direction.** Decide (a) or (b) first. With (a), change the first test to accept `np.ndarray | jax.Array` and the second to spy on the host-offload conversion differently, and add a test that a jit'd loss reading `__time__` works. Mark both as CI.","files":null}]}